NAME
    lua - Lua-syntax interpreter (AdventOS subset)

SYNOPSIS
    lua FILE.lua            run a Lua script
    lua                     interactive REPL
    lua -e "STMT"           run one statement

DESCRIPTION
    A tree-walking interpreter for a subset of Lua 5. NOT a port of
    the upstream Lua sources — this is a fresh single-file
    implementation written for AdventOS, recognizing the same
    surface syntax for the common cases.

IMPLEMENTED
    Types          nil, boolean, number (int32), string, table, function
    Statements     assignment, local, if/elseif/else, while, numeric for,
                   function definition, return, break,
                   expression-as-statement
    Expressions    binops (+ - * / % ^ == ~= < > <= >= .. and or),
                   unops (- not #), function calls f(...),
                   indexing t[k] / t.k, table constructors
                   {1,2,3} or {x=1, [k]=v}, function literals
    Built-ins      print, type, tostring, tonumber, ipairs,
                   string.len/sub/upper/lower/rep,
                   table.insert/concat,
                   io.read/write,
                   os.time/exit

NUMBERS ARE INT32
    Real Lua's numbers are doubles (or int64 in 5.3+). AdventOS user
    programs build with -mgeneral-regs-only and no FPU support, so
    floating-point would require either kernel FPU context switching
    or shipping libgcc soft-float helpers. For a shell-scripting
    language neither is worth it. Practical consequence:

      1 / 3       =>  0       (integer division)
      2 ^ 10      =>  1024    (works for nonneg integer exponents)
      1.5         =>  parse error

    Numbers fit in ~2 billion. tonumber("123") works; tonumber("1.5")
    returns nil.

DELIBERATELY NOT IMPLEMENTED
    - Metatables and __index / __newindex
    - Coroutines
    - pcall / xpcall / error (any runtime error aborts the process;
      the REPL has no fork-per-line so an error ends the session)
    - Multiple return values
    - Closures with upvalues (functions see globals + their own
      locals — a nested function can't capture an outer local)
    - require / packages
    - String patterns / gmatch (string.find isn't included)
    - math.* (no math library)

EXAMPLES
    lua -e "print('hello, world')"
    lua -e "for i=1,10 do print(i, i*i) end"
    lua /scripts/test.lua

    A short script:
      local function fact(n)
          if n <= 1 then return 1 end
          return n * fact(n - 1)
      end
      print(fact(10))            -- prints 3628800

    Tables:
      local t = { "apple", "banana", "cherry" }
      table.insert(t, "date")
      print(#t)                  -- 4
      print(t[1])                -- apple
      print(table.concat(t, ", "))

EXIT STATUS
    0   script ran to completion
    1   any error (file missing, parse error, runtime error)

SEE ALSO
    sh(1), ed(1)

NOTES
    Single-process REPL — any error from die() exits the process. A
    fork-per-line shape would recover but adds latency; not worth
    it for the daily-use case.
